// gpio -- SoC-agnostic GPIO pull-mode contract.
//
// Every GPIO-capable chip lets a pin's internal pull resistor be
// configured to roughly the same 3 states -- none, pull-down, or
// pull-up -- but the REGISTER ENCODING for those 3 states is not
// standardized even within one vendor's own product line. Two
// concrete, real examples from Dhruva OS's own multi-board port:
//
//   - Broadcom BCM2835 (Raspberry Pi 1): GPPUD register, none=0b00,
//     down=0b01, up=0b10.
//   - Broadcom BCM2711 (Raspberry Pi 4): GPIO_PUP_PDN_CNTRL register,
//     none=0b00, up=0b01, down=0b10 -- up/down SWAPPED relative to
//     BCM2835, despite being the same vendor's own later SoC.
//
// A caller that passes a raw hardware bit pattern across that boundary
// silently gets the OPPOSITE pull direction. This package exists so
// that never happens again: `gpio_pull_none/_down/_up()` are the only
// values any board's own `gpio_set_pull`/`gpio_get_pull` function
// should accept or return. Each board's own driver owns a small,
// private translation table (see kernel_main_rpi4.vani's own
// `gpio_pull_to_hw_rpi4`/`gpio_pull_from_hw_rpi4` for the BCM2711
// example) mapping these 3 canonical values to and from its own real
// register bits -- the canonical values themselves never change, so a
// brand new board (not a Raspberry Pi, not even Broadcom) only ever
// needs to write ITS OWN translation table against this same contract,
// never touch any other board's driver, and never introduce a new set
// of magic numbers for callers to get wrong.
//
// Deliberately does NOT attempt to cover GPIO function-select (input/
// output/alternate-function routing): unlike pull-mode, that
// mechanism itself varies by chip family, not just its encoding (e.g.
// BCM28xx packs a single 3-bit alt-function field per pin, while an
// STM32 GPIO uses a 2-bit mode field plus an entirely separate
// alternate-function-selection register) -- there is no single small
// contract that fits all of them, so that stays a per-board concern.
// Plain digital write/read (drive a pin high/low, read its level) are
// universal enough that they don't need a contract like this one
// either -- every board's own `gpio_write(pin, value)`/`gpio_read(pin)`
// already agree on that shape without any translation layer.

fn gpio_pull_none() -> u32 {
  return 0 as u32;
}

fn gpio_pull_down() -> u32 {
  return 1 as u32;
}

fn gpio_pull_up() -> u32 {
  return 2 as u32;
}
